{"kind":"assert","text":"Review for Hugh: stop a closing commitment from protecting its obsolete candidates.\n\nThe latest documentation merge provides a small example. Its receipt 39d66ff3 closes #19970 as satisfied and landed, yet seals the rejected first artifact #19974 (5000448e) as a protected sibling under that same request. The old artifact remains live. This follows the current rule: [Classify](internal/mergeplan/mergeplan.go@6f4864fdaf2069f9ab917286e537db05bea12f51:631) asks the [protection index](internal/mergeplan/mergeplan.go@6f4864fdaf2069f9ab917286e537db05bea12f51:906) about commitments before the merge, without considering which ones the receipt will close.\n\nThis is repeated behavior, not a one-off cleanup oversight. I checked three actual receipts and their current projected lifecycles. The binding-guard merge protected 57 candidates under the promise it closed; the I6 documentation merge protected 17 under the request it closed; the latest merge protected one. Of those 75 classifications, 71 artifacts remain live at the recorded frontier. That count is evidence of retained candidates, not proof that every one is disposable. The exact lists, receipts and closure evidence are [attached](closing-commitment-evidence.json).\n\nMy recommendation is one prospective rule correction: a lifecycle that this exact receipt will close cannot be the sole reason for protecting an outside-target candidate. Use the merge's verified implementation binding to identify those lifecycles; do not infer them from matching requester, branch or text. Another unsettled commitment still protects the candidate. So does any protection for a publication on another landed destination, which remains the separate issue in review #19530. Keep exact-path succession and carried wider pointers unchanged.\n\nWhere no remaining protection exists, seal the candidate as abandoned and let its author or a ratifier use the existing cleanup path. The merge still does not gain arbitrary retirement authority. This adds no task state or extra approval round; it makes the existing close-and-cleanup sequence agree with itself. Do not rewrite old receipts or bulk-retire the 71 artifacts on this note's authority.\n\nBefore adoption, review the classification rule and its fold validation together. Acceptance should cover the three retained examples, an unrelated unsettled commitment, a renewed lifecycle on the same request, a candidate shared by closing and nonclosing commitments, wider carried pointers, another published destination, and a refused merge that closes nothing. The intended benefit is fewer obsolete candidates surviving successful delivery and less manual rediscovery on later edits; I have not measured a time saving.\n\nThis is a recommendation for Hugh's review, not an adopted change or a finding that the three merges violated their current contract.","body":{"head":"6f4864fdaf2069f9ab917286e537db05bea12f51","stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:2242708d4264d2b4fbd4ff00c8dab19a89e5f2fd, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:2f0fdba195b48c567ce5db78d95afe539b02ee84, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:39d66ff37d57ff1a1ade39374a4f7c0c2fffc50a, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:b098c92f86316b8d042cfba8f8e2d177850738dc stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:03270b167c8f14b55a31919b2489715ee513b20f, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:08665b2b9d366fcd6ec3094785ad68eb441b1fcd, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:207fceae71ec15f3f8e1d046517d1a4f446532bd, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:384d404a337eb651aae81c370174a031ab08982e and 26 more"}}